  "notes": [
    "Tail_Anchor's key/anchor pool (200 rows) is a ROUTING pool, not a class-indexed prototype list: the official forward routes over ALL 200 slots regardless of nb_classes, any slot can receive mass and be optimized, so the diagnostic keeps the full pool",
    "routing similarity is taken BEFORE anchor selection; the anchor component of the interface never enters the routing math",
    "flip rates are conditioned on the OLD interface's top-1 slot belonging to the group; argmax is tau-invariant so flip rates are constant across tau rows",
    "per-column JS: slot j's profile = R[:, j] renormalized over reference samples; JS = sqrt(JSD) between the two interfaces",
    "reference inputs are public surrogate data (kd_fusion_prompt protocol), deterministic per-client sample; identical batch composition in both interface passes (batchwise prompt voting cancels)",
    "protection set is ALWAYS replicated at the A3 snapshot moment (tasks 0..old_task top-1 under the old key, ViT task_id=old_task+1, batch=16 hardcoded as in the official A2); the a2_state stored in old_ckpt is one task stale and is recorded but never used",
    "MECHANISTIC NOTE: in the official phase-2 loss the key's ONLY continuous gradient channel is -0.1*pull + Adam weight decay \u2014 CE and InfoNCE reach the key only through the discrete topk index. lambda_grad (base/key-gradient norm vs KL/key-gradient norm) is therefore the primary lambda estimate; lambda_loss (CE/KL) is a coarse reference only",
    "lambda decisions should use ADJACENT checkpoint pairs (one task of drift, e.g. task_03_end -> task_04_end): A3 snapshots R_old at every task start. Multi-task spans remain useful as accumulated-drift diagnosis",
    "lambda_grad balances gradient norms at one point (the new_ckpt interface); Adam normalizes per-parameter update magnitudes, so treat lambda_grad as the CENTER of the lambda sweep grid (scan at least +-1 order of magnitude), never as an exact training-scale equivalence",
    "the floored KL variant (lambda_grad_floor) evaluates a DIFFERENT objective, KL(R_old^{1e-6} || R_new^{1e-4}): the teacher keeps the official 1e-6 normalizer while the student-side normalizer is floored at 1e-4, and rows below the floor shift the softmax denominator \u2014 a ZERO-drift configuration (identical features and keys) yields nonzero loss and key gradient from the normalization mismatch alone. It is a normalization-rule sensitivity experiment only, NEVER a lambda recommendation for the original KL; training it would require an independent variant design",
    "pooled row-grad shares (*_pooled) are L1/squared-norm shares of the merged per-client row-grad vector \u2014 a different aggregation than the pooled lambda (a ratio of client-mean gradient norms), and not the contribution rate of the federated-average gradient either",
    "unit-row-norm counterfactual (kl_grad_norm_prot_unitnorm / kl_grad_unitnorm_ratio): the STUDENT key rows are rescaled to unit norm while the teacher keeps the official rule. For rows >= 1e-6 the forward routing is EXACTLY unchanged (cosine routing depends only on direction), so the gradient drop isolates the 1/||k|| Jacobian amplification of l2_normalize \u2014 a parameterization artifact, not constraint strength. Rows below the 1e-6 floor (n_key_rows_below_1e-6_student) are NOT forward-equivalent under the counterfactual (their official sims are shrunk by ||k||/1e-6; the counterfactual restores full unit length and shifts the softmax denominator), so no strict forward-equivalence claim is made for them. A diagnostic of scale degeneracy only \u2014 NOT a training reparameterization proposal",
    "grad_cos_base_kl_prot: cosine between the instantaneous base key gradient (-0.1*grad pull + wd*K on ONE train batch \u2014 the key's ONLY continuous training channel; CE/InfoNCE pass no continuous key gradient) and the protected-sample KL gradient. <0 conflict / ~0 orthogonal / >0 synergy. A one-batch snapshot, not a training-averaged direction",
    "protected_sample_coverage = n_prot_samples / n_ref: when the client-mean coverage exceeds 0.95 the sample mask degenerates to a near-global constraint on surrogate data and the protected-vs-unprotected flip comparison is UNINFORMATIVE \u2014 the small number of historical protected slots absorbing nearly all surrogate routing mass is itself a strong routing-concentration observation, but it is NOT mask separation evidence"
  ],
